<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Serpukhov</h1><p class="st-meta">1341 CE – 1456 CE (approx.) · capital Serpukhov</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An appanage of the Moscow princes south of the capital, ruled by Vladimir the Bold, hero of the Battle of Kulikovo.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oka-river">Oka</a> guarded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> southern approaches against the steppe. In 1341 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan I Kalita's</a> will took effect, and his youngest son Andrei received Serpukhov and surrounding lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borovsk">Borovsk</a>, as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> within the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Andrei's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-bold">Vladimir the Bold</a>, cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> leading general. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Kulikovo</a> in 1380 he and the commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-bobrok-volynsky">Dmitry Bobrok</a> led the ambush regiment whose attack decided the battle. He built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a> kremlin and founded monasteries, and his principality served as a buffer against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> and Lithuanian raids.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow's</a> civil war Vladimir's grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-yaroslavich-of-serpukhov">Vasily Yaroslavich</a> supported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II</a>, but in 1456 the grand prince had him arrested and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serpukhov">Serpukhov</a>. Vasily died in prison, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> ended.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1341 CE – 1456 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.39%;width:2.29%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1341 CE – 1456 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Serpukhov</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Andrei of Serpukhov, Vladimir the Bold, Vasily Yaroslavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Serpukhov-Borovsk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=principality-of-serpukhov" aria-label="Principality of Serpukhov on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5501 300 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Serpukhov lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5821 556L5820 533"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5820 533h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5821 556h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5821 556h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Serpukhov. <a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=principality-of-serpukhov">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
